{#
  A primary ClusterUserDefinedNetwork per tenant.

  Layer3 topology with role Primary: this becomes THE network for pods in the
  selected namespaces - their eth0, their default gateway, their isolation
  boundary. It is not a secondary attachment.

  The subnet depends on which phase this is rendered for, and the difference
  matters:

    shared  (phase 2, targetVRF unset)    -> udn_subnet_shared, unique per
            tenant. Both tenants' routes end up in one VRF on the node and on
            the leaf, where one prefix cannot mean two things - and nothing
            stops you: OVN-Kubernetes does not validate this, so the CR is
            Accepted and one tenant is quietly unreachable. main.yml asserts
            the subnets differ, because the cluster will not.

    vrflite (phase 3, targetVRF: auto)    -> udn_subnet, deliberately
            IDENTICAL between tenants. Each tenant's routes live only in its
            own VRF, so the same addresses can exist twice. Proving that is
            most of the point of VRF-Lite, and it is the one result that
            cannot be faked by a phase-2 setup that happens to work.

  A namespace only joins this network if it carries the matching label AND
  the label is present when the namespace is created - OVN-Kubernetes binds a
  primary network at namespace creation time, so labelling an existing
  namespace does nothing. The workload tasks create the namespaces fresh for
  that reason.
#}
apiVersion: k8s.ovn.org/v1
kind: ClusterUserDefinedNetwork
metadata:
  name: {{ tenant.name }}
  labels:
    {{ udn_bgp_cudn_label_key }}: "enabled"
    udn-lab-phase: {{ udn_phase }}
spec:
  namespaceSelector:
    matchLabels:
      udn-tenant: {{ tenant.name }}
{% set subnet = tenant.udn_subnet_shared if udn_phase == 'shared' else tenant.udn_subnet %}
{% if tenant.topology | default('Layer3') == 'Layer2' %}
  network:
    topology: Layer2
    layer2:
      role: Primary
      # One flat broadcast domain across every node, so the subnet is NOT
      # sliced per node the way Layer3 slices it - the whole prefix exists
      # everywhere. That is what lets a workload keep its address when it
      # moves between nodes, and it is why this is the topology for VMs.
      #
      # It changes what BGP sees, too: every node advertises this same
      # prefix, so the leaf gets one prefix with three paths rather than a
      # distinct per-node prefix from each. Both are correct; only the
      # Layer3 one looks like what a router operator expects.
      subnets:
        - {{ subnet }}
      # The reason this topology is here. Persistent ties the address
      # allocation to the VM rather than to the pod backing it, so it
      # survives the pod being replaced - which is exactly what a live
      # migration does: a new virt-launcher pod on the target node takes
      # over. Without it the VM comes up on the other side with a different
      # address, which is not a migration anyone wants. It requires subnets
      # to be set, which they are above.
      ipam:
        lifecycle: Persistent
{% else %}
  network:
    topology: Layer3
    layer3:
      role: Primary
      subnets:
        - cidr: {{ subnet }}
          # Per-node slice of the tenant subnet. /24 out of a /16 gives 254
          # pods per node and 256 nodes, which is far past what this lab
          # needs and keeps the advertised prefixes readable in FRR.
          hostSubnet: {{ tenant.udn_host_subnet }}
{% endif %}
